# Herdr workflows

## Choose Herdr only for visible, persistent work

Use Herdr when the user wants to watch or attach to a long-running server, test run, build, or explicitly visible worker. For short-lived research/review fanout, use OMP's built-in `task` tool instead; do not create Herdr panes just to parallelize routine work.

Before controlling Herdr, verify that this OMP session was launched by Herdr:

```bash
echo "HERDR_ENV=${HERDR_ENV:-unset}"
```

If `HERDR_ENV` is not `1`, do not try Herdr commands; explain that this session lacks the Herdr connection.

## Inspect before acting

```bash
herdr pane list
herdr workspace list
herdr tab list --workspace 1
```

Workspace IDs, tab IDs, and pane IDs can compact after closes. Re-read them before using a remembered ID. Use `--no-focus` for creates/splits unless the user requests a focus change.

## Run visible work
For a long-running command in a new visible pane:

```bash
NEW_PANE=$(herdr pane split 1-1 --direction right --no-focus | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["pane"]["pane_id"])')
herdr pane run "$NEW_PANE" "<command>"
herdr pane read "$NEW_PANE" --source recent --lines 40
```

For a named, visible OMP worker when the user asks for one:

```bash
herdr agent start worker --cwd "$PWD" -- omp "<task>"
herdr wait agent-status worker --status done --timeout 300000
herdr pane list
```

Read the completed pane's output and report its result; Herdr panes do not deliver results into this OMP conversation automatically.

## Wait and inspect

```bash
herdr wait output <pane-id> --match "ready" --timeout 30000
herdr pane read <pane-id> --source recent --lines 80
herdr pane read <pane-id> --source recent-unwrapped --lines 80
```

`recent-unwrapped` is useful when matching text that wraps in the terminal. A wait timeout exits non-zero; inspect the pane instead of claiming completion.

## Manage panes and workspaces

```bash
herdr pane send-keys <pane-id> Enter
herdr pane send-text <pane-id> "<text>"
herdr pane close <pane-id>
herdr tab create --workspace <workspace-id> --label "tests" --no-focus
herdr workspace create --cwd "$PWD" --label "api-server" --no-focus
```

Re-list IDs after closing anything. Keep user-visible work running only as long as it remains useful; do not close or overwrite a user's existing pane without permission.
